  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe primary bottleneck in tackling the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem is the high-dimensional optimization landscape with up to 60 variables, which limits the exploration reach of local optimizers like SLSQP. This can trap the algorithm in suboptimal valleys. To overcome this, we introduce a multi-resolution strategy with dimensionality reduction. We represent h(x) as a piecewise-constant function with a sparse support of active intervals, significantly reducing the problem dimension from n_points to a small number of pulse parameters (mean:3, max:4 pulses). This allows us to:\n1. Escalate search across resolutions (40,60,90,120 samples)\n2. Explore function space efficiently with SLEP (Simulated Localized Exploration Process)\n3. Ensure feasibility with projected gradients\n4. Merge solutions across scales to achieve lower overlap bounds\n\nThe mathematical formulation reduces execution time by orders of magnitude while still converging to tight bounds. By limiting pulse auto-overlaps and including derivative-based repair mechanisms, we maintain physical plausibility in the function space exploration.",
